  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nWednesday: Positioning of decent 850/700 theta-e ridge axis, \nand the proximity of a more decent shortwave trough aloft near \npeak heating, at least for late July, and the Precipitable Water\n(PWAT) values from 1.5 to 2 inches, along with impressive shear\nand instability suggests another round of scattered to numerous\nafternoon and evening showers and scattered thunderstorms, with\na marginal risk of severe thunderstorms to the right of a line \nfrom Laramie to Casper in the afternoon and evening from the \nmeteorologists at the SPC, Storm Prediction Center. \n\nThursday through Monday: Monsoonal moisture retreats out of our\nCounty Warning Area (CWA) as a drier and hotter air mass \nreturns producing nearly dry conditions with only minimal \nchances for isolated to scattered thunderstorms over and near \nour Snowy, Sierra Madre, and Laramie Ranges due to differential\norographic processes. Temperatures will soar once again, \nespecially for Sunday and Monday, with some locations possibly \napproaching near record maximum temperatures, and perhaps the \nlong standing all time record high temperature of 100 degrees at\nCheyenne, that has been achieved four times, the most recent in\nthe 1950s.",
